14. LANGUAGE SWITCHING

Implement functional KG and RU language switching.

The default language should be Kyrgyz.

When RU is selected, translate the website interface and invitation content into natural Russian.

When KG is selected, display the original Kyrgyz content.

Requirements:

- All section headings must change.
- All wedding program event titles must change.
- Calendar controls must change.
- Countdown labels must change.
- Address and invitation text must change.
- Buttons must change.
- Preserve the couple's names and date.
- Preserve the same design in both languages.
- Remember the selected language during the current browsing session.

Do not create decorative language buttons that do nothing.

15. MUSIC PLAYER

Implement a working music player.

Requirements:

- Use the user's supplied music file when available.
- Put the file in a clearly named public/assets/music folder.
- Start playback after the visitor opens the envelope.
- Add a visible play/pause control.
- Keep music playing while scrolling between sections.
- Avoid creating multiple simultaneous audio instances.
- Handle missing audio files and playback errors gracefully.

If no music file has been provided, create a clearly documented asset location and explain where the user should add the file.

Never pretend music is playing when the audio file is missing.

16. TECHNICAL STACK

Use a modern, maintainable frontend architecture.

Preferred stack:

- React
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS or well-organized CSS modules
- Framer Motion for animations, if useful
- Lucide React for interface icons

Use only dependencies that are necessary.

If the existing GitHub repository already contains a working framework, inspect it first and preserve the appropriate project structure rather than replacing it unnecessarily.

Implement reusable components such as:

- EnvelopeOpening
- LanguageSwitcher
- MusicPlayer
- WeddingHero
- InvitationMessage
- WeddingCalendar
- CountdownTimer
- WeddingTimeline
- BanquetSection
- AddressSection
- RestaurantGallery
- WeddingFooter

Keep wedding information in a central configuration file so names, dates, times, translations, and images can be updated easily.

17. RESPONSIVE DESIGN

The website must work well on:

- Android phones.
- iPhones.
- Tablets.
- Desktop browsers.
- Windows 11 computers.

Mobile is the primary target.

Requirements:

- Mobile-first layout.
- No horizontal scrolling.
- Readable typography.
- Correct image scaling.
- Comfortable touch targets.
- Smooth vertical scrolling.
- Responsive timeline cards.
- Appropriate spacing on small screens.
- Reduced-motion support for users who prefer fewer animations.

The site should look like a premium wedding invitation, not an ordinary business website.

18. ACCESSIBILITY AND PERFORMANCE

Implement:

- Semantic HTML.
- Accessible button labels.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Visible focus states.
- Alternative text for images.
- Appropriate color contrast.
- Reduced-motion support.
- Optimized image loading.
- Lazy loading for below-the-fold images.
- Correct page metadata.
- A suitable browser title: "Алмаз & Анел — Үйлөнүү тоюна чакыруу".

Do not lazy-load the primary hero image if doing so would delay the opening experience.

19. TESTING AND QUALITY CONTROL

Before declaring the project complete:

1. Install the required dependencies.
2. Run the production build.
3. Fix TypeScript errors.
4. Fix build errors.
5. Test all sections.
6. Verify the countdown against November 16, 2026, at 16:00 Bishkek time.
7. Verify the November 2026 calendar grid.
8. Verify the calendar download.
9. Test KG/RU language switching.
10. Test envelope opening.
11. Test music playback after user interaction.
12. Test the music play/pause control.
13. Verify all restaurant images load.
14. Verify the map button opens the correct address search.
15. Check for mobile horizontal overflow.
16. Check that no buttons are decorative-only when they are supposed to work.
17. Check for console errors.
18. Verify that all supplied Kyrgyz text is preserved correctly.

Do not mark tests as passed unless they have actually been run.

20. GITHUB AND DEPLOYMENT

Inspect the current repository before making changes.

If the repository is empty, create the complete project structure.

Implement the entire application and organize its files logically.

Include a README.md containing:

- Project overview.
- Technology stack.
- Installation instructions.
- Local development instructions.
- Production build instructions.
- Instructions for replacing restaurant photographs.
- Instructions for adding the music file.
- Instructions for updating the wedding date and text.
- Instructions for deploying the site.

Prepare the project for deployment on GitHub Pages or another compatible static hosting service.

If GitHub Pages is selected, configure the correct Vite base path and deployment workflow where appropriate.

Do not claim that the website is deployed unless deployment has actually succeeded.

21. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The project is complete only when:

- The complete invitation website has been implemented.
- The design closely follows the supplied visual references.
- The opening envelope works.
- Music starts when the envelope is opened, provided the audio file is available.
- The countdown uses the correct wedding date and time.
- The calendar displays the correct date.
- All five wedding program events are present.
- The restaurant photographs are integrated.
- The address and map button work.
- KG/RU switching works.
- The website is responsive.
- The production build succeeds.
- The README explains how to run and customize the project.

Do not stop after producing a plan or a partial mockup.

Implement the full working website, run the available checks, fix discovered issues, and provide a concise summary of the files created, test results, and deployment steps.

Use the uploaded reference screenshots as the design source of truth. Preserve the wedding details exactly as specified above.
